  "holistic_F_N": 2,
  "holistic_F_H": -2,
  "section_notes": "The final rules impose mandatory reporting of confirmed incidents, a 5% thermal-test failure cap with a 36-month transition for already-marketed models, remote shut-off within 3 years for new units and 6 years for retrofits, built-in fire suppression at 36 months, and a mandatory 8/100k recall trigger with customer notices. Northwind secured longer S3 deadlines and avoids the S5 recall, while Halcyon secures the strict S2 cap and purchaser-level test-result disclosure; the S5 trigger remains a major burden for Halcyon's Ember 8."
 },
